\par     }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\ul\cf5\insrsid15008363 REFERENCE
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 METHODS OF MEASURING GDP
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 There are three equivalent ways of measuring GDP:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 i. The product or value added method }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 which sums the value added by all the productive
\par entities in the economy;
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 ii. The expenditure method }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 which sums up the value of all the \'93final goods\'94 transactions
\par taking place in the economy;
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 iii. The factor income method }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 which sums up all the incomes earned by all the factors of
\par production in the economy (rent for land, wages for labour, interest for capital, and equity
\par returns for entrepreneurship).
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs28\insrsid15008363 Value added }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is the difference between the value of goods produced and the cost of materials and
\par supplies used in producing them. Value added consists of the wages, interest and profit components
\par added to the output by a firm. Value added is the difference between inputs and outputs e-g if a firm
\par spends Rs.500 making a good (inputs) and sells its product (output) for Rs.750, then value added is
\par Rs.250.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\insrsid15008363 
\par 
\par Question No: 53    ( Marks: 5 ) 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid13390255 
