\b\f1\insrsid15008363  Differentiate between inflation and deflation.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 ANSWER:
\par Inflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is a situation in which there is a continuous rise in the general price level. 
\par WHEREAS }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 Deflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is the
\par opposite of inflation and occurs when the general level of prices falls.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\insrsid15008363 Inflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 
is when the money supply increases by more than the real economy does. This means that one unit of currency will buy less than it could in the past. \line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs28\insrsid15008363 Deflation}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid15008363  is when the money supply contracts, making a unit of currency able to buy more than it could in the past. This is the exact opposite of inflation, but it is also bad.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid15008363 
