\pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls2\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid15008363 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 An increase in wage rates 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af3 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid15008363 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn 
\pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\faauto\ls2\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid15008363 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 An increase in the prices of raw materials 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 These sources of a decrease in aggregate supply operate by increasing costs, and the resulting inflation is called }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid15008363 cost-push inflation.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 4.   }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
  What are the comparative advantages between countries according Hechshcer-Ohlin? And criticisms against it. 
\par 
\par ANSWER:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 According to Hechshcer-Ohlin suppose we are given two countries one abundant
\par in labour and one abundant in capital and a labour-intensive good and a capital intensive
\par good, the labour abundant country will have comparative advantage in the production of the labour-intensive good while the capital abundant country will have comparative advantage in the capital-intensive good.
\par So the determinants of comparative advantage among countries according to Hechshcer-Ohlin is capital goods and labour intensive goods.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\cf1\insrsid15008363 Critics argued}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 
 that comparative advantage can and should be viewed in a dynamic (time-varying) sense, and that it was not wise to rule out the possibility of Pakistan developing comparative advantage in cars at some future point in time.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\cf1\insrsid15008363 Consequent:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\ul\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs22\cf1\insrsid15008363 The major criticism leveled against Hechscher-Ohlin type trade theories are that they views
\par comparative advantage in an essentially static sense; i.e. if Pakistan is better at producing
\par cotton and Japan better at producing, then this situation will always prevail}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 \line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 1.components of current account of BOPS:
\par ANSWER:
\par 
\par Following are the components:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 1.GOODS
\par 2.SERVICES
\par 3.INCOME
\par 4.CURRENT ACCOUNT.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\cf6\insrsid15008363 (FOR COMPLETE UNDERSTANDING AND MORE COMPONANTS VISIT THIS LINK)
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 http://www.investopedia.com/articles/03/061803.asp}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 \line :\line 1}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 . Difference between capital deepening and capital widening(5 MARKS)
\par ANSWER:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs32\ul\insrsid15008363 Capital deepening
\par  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is a term used in economics to describe an economy where capital per worker is increasing. It is an increase in the
\par capital intensity. Capital deepening is often measured by the capital stock per labour hour.Overall, the
\par economy will expand, and productivity per worker will increase. However, economic expansion will not
\par continue indefinitely through capital deepening alone. This is partly due to diminishing returns and wear
\par & tear
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs32\ul\cf1\insrsid15008363 capital widening:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is a term used to describe the situation where capital stock is increasing at the
\par same rate as the labour force, thus capital per worker remains constant. The economy will expand in
\par terms of aggregate output, but productivity per worker will remain constant
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 \line 2 .Difference between GNP and NNP:(5 MARKS)
\par ANSWER:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 Gross National Product (GNP):
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 Gross national product (GNP) is the value, at current market prices, of all final goods and services
\par produced during a year by the factors owned by the citizens of a country. Thus the income earned by
\par Pakistani citizens working in the US would be included in Pakistan\rquote s GNP but excluded from Pakistan\rquote s
\par GDP. Conversely, the income earned by a US citizen (individual or corporate) in Pakistan would be
\par included in Pakistan\rquote s GDP but excluded from Pakistan\rquote s GNP. Generally, GNP = GDP + net factor
\par income from abroad. The abbreviation for gross national product, which is the total market value of all
\par goods and services produced by the citizens of an economy during a given period of time, usually one
\par year. Gross national product often was once the federal government's official measure of how much
\par output our economy produces.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 GNP = GDP + Net factor incomes from abroad
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 Net National Product (NNP):
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 Mathematically, national income is net national product (NNP). It is GNP adjusted for depreciation. In
\par words, it is the net output of commodities and services flowing during the year from the country\rquote s
\par production system in the hands of ultimate consumers. The total market value of all final goods and
\par services produced by citizens of an economy during a given period of time, usually a year, after
\par adjusting for the depreciation of capital. Net national product, abbreviated NNP, has the same relation to
\par net domestic product (NDP) as gross national product (GNP) has to gross domestic product (GDP). Net
\par national product also has the same relation to gross national product that net domestic product has to
\par gross domestic product. Like NDP, NNP is a measure of the net production in the economy.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 NNP = GNP \endash  Depreciation allowance}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs32\ul\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 3. Explain Comparative advantage among contries according to Hechshcer-Ohlin. And criricism on it?
\par ANSWER:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 According to Hechshcer-Ohlin suppose we are given two countries one abundant
\par in labour and one abundant in capital and a labour-intensive good and a capital intensive
\par good, the labour abundant country will have comparative advantage in the production of the labour-intensive good while the capital abundant country will have comparative advantage in the capital-intensive good.
\par So the determinants of comparative advantage among countries according to Hechshcer-Ohlin is capital goods and labour intensive goods.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\cf1\insrsid15008363 Critics argued}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 
 that comparative advantage can and should be viewed in a dynamic (time-varying) sense, and that it was not wise to rule out the possibility of Pakistan developing comparative advantage in cars at some future point in time.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\cf1\insrsid15008363 Consequent:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\ul\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs22\cf1\insrsid15008363 The major criticism leveled against Hechscher-Ohlin type trade theories are that they views
\par comparative advantage in an essentially static sense; i.e. if Pakistan is better at producing
\par cotton and Japan better at producing, then this situation will always prevail.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 Q # 1    Difference between inflation and deflation? Marks 3
\par ANSWER:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 Inflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is a situation in which there is a continuous rise in the general price level. 
\par WHEREAS }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 Deflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is the
\par opposite of inflation and occurs when the general level of prices falls.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\insrsid15008363 Inflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 
is when the money supply increases by more than the real economy does. This means that one unit of currency will buy less than it could in the past. \line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs28\insrsid15008363 Deflation}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid15008363  is when the money supply contracts, making a unit of currency able to buy more than it could in the past. This is the exact opposite of inflation, but it is also bad.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 Q # 2    How the central banks plays its important role in order to control the activities of commercial banks?  Marks 3
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\cf1\insrsid15008363 central bank}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\cf1\insrsid15008363 reserve bank}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 , or }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\cf1\insrsid15008363 monetary authority}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363  is a banking institution granted the exclusive privilege to lend a government it
s currency. Like a normal commercial bank, a central bank charges interest on the loans made to borrowers, primarily the government of whichever country the bank exists for, and to other commercial banks, typically as a 'lender of last resort'. However, a
 
central bank is distinguished from a normal commercial bank because it has a monopoly on creating the currency of that nation, which is loaned to the government in the form of legal tender. It is a bank that can lend money to other banks in times of need.
 Its primary function is to provide the nation's }{\field{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 HYPERLINK "/wiki/Money_supply"}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363\charrsid15008363 {\*\datafield 
00d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b0200000001000000e0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b260000002f00770069006b0069002f004d006f006e00650079005f0073007500700070006c007900000000}}}{\fldrslt {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 money supply}}}
\sectd \ltrsect\linex0\sectdefaultcl\sftnbj {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid15008363 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par  
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 Q # 3    Difference between depreciation and appreciation of currency? Marks 5
\par 
\par  ANSWER:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs32\ul\insrsid15008363 Depreciation:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 This is a more or less permanent decrease in value or price. "More or less permanent" doesn't include
\par temporary, short-term drops in price that are common in many markets. It's only those price declines
\par that reflect a reduction in consumer satisfaction.While all sorts of stuff can depreciate in value, some of
\par the more common ones are capital, real estate, corporate stock, and money
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af43\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f43\fs28\ul\insrsid15008363 APPRECIATION}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 
\par This is a more or less permanent increase in value or price. "More or less permanent" doesn't include
\par temporary, short-term jumps in price that are common in many markets. Appreciation is only those
\par price increases that reflect greater consumer satisfaction and thus value. While all sorts of stuff can
\par appreciate in value, some of the more common ones are real estate, works of art, corporate stock, and
\par money.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par  Q # 4    what is meant by laffer curve? Also explain the shape of this curve? Marks 5
\par ANSWER:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 The graphical inverted-U relation between tax rates and total tax collections by government is known as
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af43\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f43\fs28\insrsid15008363 Laffer curve}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 . This curve is developed by economist Arthur Laffer; the Laffer curve formed a key
\par theoretical foundation for supply-side economics of President Reagan during the 1980s. It is based on
\par the notion that government collects zero revenue if the tax rate is 0% and if the tax rate is 100%. At a
\par 100% tax rate no one has the incentive to work, produce, and earn income, so there is no income to tax.
\par As such, the optimum tax rate, in which government revenue is maximized, lies somewhere between 0%
\par and 100%. This generates a curve shaped like an inverted U, rising from zero to a peak, and then falling
\par back to zero. If the economy is operating to the right of the peak, then government revenue can be
\par increased by decreasing the tax rate.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf6\insrsid15008363 (AGAIN FOR DIAGRAM VISIT PAGE NO 175 OF CHP # 40)
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363  
\par 
\par   }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs28\cf1\insrsid15008363 A slope of consumption function is less than 1." What is meant by this statement?}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs22\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par ANSWER:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs22\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par When we drawn in expenditure income space, the consumption function plots as a straight line with positive intercept, and a positive but less than 1 slope. The slope is less than 1 because not all the income is consumed some part of the income is saved.}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par  Differentiate between Inflation and deflation.                               Marks3
\par 
\par ANSWER:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 Inflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is a situation in which there is a continuous rise in the general price level. 
\par WHEREAS }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af44\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f44\fs22\insrsid15008363 Deflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af43\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f43\fs22\insrsid15008363 is the
\par opposite of inflation and occurs when the general level of prices falls.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\insrsid15008363 Inflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 is when the money supply increases by more than the real economy does. This means that one unit of currency will buy less than it
 could in the past. \line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs28\insrsid15008363 Deflation}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid15008363 
 is when the money supply contracts, making a unit of currency able to buy more than it could in the past. This is the exact opposite of inflation, but it is also bad}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid15008363 
\par 
